;; SCRATCH-C-TILE matmul — numeric proof, and the clause-ordering bug it exposed.
;;
;; No MMA anywhere in this kernel: a plain scalar tiled matmul whose C-tile is an ordinary SLM
;; scratch matrix.  It is here because Chris pointed at 135/01 and said "I don't see anything
;; about the kernel algorithm that would suggest it is not differentiable" — and framed the
;; answer: scratch tiles become IMPLICIT KERNEL ARGS, so the signature is really
;; (A-tile B-tile C-tile A B &out C).  Every construct in it is bread-and-butter AD that
;; endeavor 111 already handles.  He was right; the skip reason was a symptom I had never
;; diagnosed.
;;
;; THE BUG.  `%handle-single-value-backward` tested the record-ACCESSOR clause BEFORE the
;; gradient-inert skip list.  `extents~` ends in a tilde, so %is-accessor-p claimed it and
;; %handle-accessor-backward minted a SCALAR adjoint for its source.  On a scratch tile that
;; scalar `<tile>_ADJ` collided with the TENSOR `<tile>_ADJ` from scratch-adj-bindings — and
;; since Crisp's LET is let*-like, the scalar (bound second) SHADOWED the tensor:
;;
;;     (C-TILE_ADJ 0.0)                                    <- scalar, shadows the matrix
;;     (SET! C-TILE_ADJ (+ C-TILE_ADJ %ANF-T-1_ADJ))
;;
;; Every later `(~ C-TILE_ADJ i j)` was then indexing a float, which is all the errors
;; "No matching function overload for '~' / 'EXTENTS~' with argument types (FLOAT ...)" ever
;; meant.  It hit ANY differentiable kernel that reads a scratch tile's extents — that is,
;; every tile-stride matmul, since the expansion reads extents~ to size the grid.  Fixed by
;; testing gradient-inertness FIRST: inertness must win over shape-based dispatch.
;;
;; WHAT THE NUMERIC CHECK THEN FOUND — BUG 037.  With the crash fixed, the kernel compiles and
;; runs and returns a gradient of exactly 0.0 (the finite difference correctly gives 0.06).  A
;; backward kernel replays the forward's BINDINGS but not its STATEMENTS: `make-scratch-matrix`
;; is a binding so the tiles exist, but `load-tile-at` — which FILLS them — is a statement and is
;; never replayed, so the staged tiles are EMPTY in the backward.  dA needs B's primal values and
;; reads zeros.  Bisected: overwrite, accumulate, and three-deep loops with ONE tile operand all
;; give exact gradients; only TWO tile operands fail, because only then is a primal needed.
;; 145's MMA path dodges this by reading the ORIGINAL GLOBAL operands instead of the staged
;; tiles.  See plan/bugs.md #037.
;;
;; This spec is kept as the record: compiling is NOT differentiating.
;;
;; COMPILE-WITH[--ir-target=spv --differentiate]: PASS
;; VERIFY-AUTODIFF: A=4x4@0:0.01 B=4x4@0:0.01 at.A=1,0 h=0.5 atol=1e-3 expect.A=0.06 output-mat=4x4

(def-type mat-t (matrix float :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :row-major))

(def-kernel scratch_tile_matmul (A B &out C)
  (declare #'(mat-t mat-t &out mat-t)
           (global-size :set-to 1)
           (local-size  :set-to 1))
  (let ((A-tile (make-scratch-matrix float (4 4)))
        (B-tile (make-scratch-matrix float (4 4)))
        (C-tile (make-scratch-matrix float (4 4)))
        (K      (inner-dimension A B)))
    (matrix-multiply-tile-stride C C-tile K 4 (grid-y grid-x grid-k)
      ;; The macro does NOT auto-reset a SCRATCH C-tile — the user owns init (135/01's
      ;; documented contract, and why BUG 036's fix is register-tile-only).
      (when (= grid-k (to-ulong 0))
        (dotimes (i 4)
          (dotimes (j 4)
            (set! (~ C-tile i j) 0.0))))
      (load-tile A A-tile (grid-y grid-k))
      (load-tile B B-tile (grid-k grid-x))
      (dotimes (i 4)
        (dotimes (j 4)
          (dotimes (kk 4)
            (set! (~ C-tile i j)
                  (+ (~ C-tile i j)
                     (* (~ A-tile i kk) (~ B-tile kk j)))))))
      :epilogue
      (store-tile C-tile C (grid-y grid-x)))))
